\item{forestPlotHeterogeneityTest, }{Include the test of the residual heterogeneity in the model information section.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{forestPlotHeterogeneityTestLRT, }{Include the likelihood ratio test of the residual heterogeneity in the model information section.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{forestPlotHeterogeneityTestWald, }{Include the Wald-type test of the residual heterogeneity in the model information section.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{forestPlotModelInformation, }{Add meta-analytic model information to the forest plot.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{forestPlotPredictionIntervals, }{Include prediction intervals of the estimated marginal means and the model information output.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{forestPlotPublicationBiasTest, }{Include the publication bias test in the model information section.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{forestPlotStudyInformation, }{Add study-level information panel to the forest plot. There are three sections of the study-level information panel: a) the left section with study labels, names, and etc designed via the 'Selected Variables' option, b) the middle section visualizing the estimates and confidence intervals based on the meta-analytic input, c) the right section textually summarizing the estimates and confidence intervals based on the meta-analytic input.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{forestPlotStudyInformationAggregateBy, }{Select the variable by which the study-level information panel is aggregated.}

\item{forestPlotStudyInformationAggregateMethod, }{Select the method for aggregating the estimates.
\itemize{
  \item \code{"boxplot"}: Aggregate the estimates by boxplot.
  \item \code{"bubbles"}: Aggregate the estimates by bubbles.
}}

\item{forestPlotStudyInformationBoxplotWeightsNormalized, }{Normalize displayed user-supplied weights to sum to 100.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{forestPlotStudyInformationBoxplotWeightsPercentage, }{Display user-supplied weights as percentages.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{forestPlotStudyInformationOrderAscending, }{Order the study-level information panel in ascending order.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{forestPlotStudyInformationOrderBy, }{Select the variable by which the study-level information panel is ordered.}

\item{forestPlotStudyInformationPredictedEffects, }{Include predicted effect sizes in the middle section of the study-level information panel. Unavailable when the panel is aggregated.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{forestPlotStudyInformationSecondaryConfidenceInterval, }{Include secondary confidence interval for effect sizes in the middle section of the study-level information panel.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{forestPlotStudyInformationSelectedVariables, }{Select variables containing study-level information to be printed in the left section of the panel.}

\item{forestPlotStudyInformationSelectedVariablesSettings, }{Adjust the Title, Width, and Alignment of each column in the left section.}

\item{forestPlotStudyInformationStudyWeights, }{Include study weights in the right section of the study-level information panel. For multilevel/multivariate meta-analysis, diagonal model weights are shown, matching the default forest plot point-size weights.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{forestPlotSubgroupFullDatasetEstimatedMarginalMeans, }{Include the full dataset estimated marginal means in the forest plot if subgroups are specified.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{forestPlotSubgroupFullDatasetModelInformation, }{Include the full dataset model information in the forest plot if subgroups are specified.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{forestPlotSubgroupPanelsWithinSubgroup, }{Group the output panels within their subgroup membership.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{forestPlotSubgroupShowTitles, }{Show the subgroup heading labels in the forest plot.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{forestPlotTestsInRightPanel, }{Move test results text to the right panel.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{heterogeneityGamma, }{Include γ, the multiplicative heterogeneity of the unrestricted weighted least squares model, i.e., the factor by which the sampling variances are multiplied.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{heterogeneityH2, }{Include H², an index indicating the ratio of total variability to sampling variability.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{heterogeneityI2, }{Include I², the percentage of total variation across studies due to heterogeneity.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{heterogeneityModelIncludeIntercept, }{Include an intercept in the heterogeneity model.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{heterogeneityModelLink, }{Link function used in the heterogeneity model: 'log' or 'identity'.}

\item{heterogeneityModelTerms, }{Variables assigned as model terms in the heterogeneity model. Specifying a heterogeneity model results in a 'location-scale' meta-analytic model.}

\item{heterogeneitySqrtGamma, }{Include √γ, the square root of the multiplicative heterogeneity of the unrestricted weighted least squares model.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{heterogeneityTau, }{Include 𝜏, the square root of the estimated between-study variance.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{heterogeneityTau2, }{Include 𝜏², the estimated between-study variance.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{includeFullDatasetInSubgroupAnalysis, }{Include the full dataset output in the subgroup analysis. This option is only available when the subgroup analysis is selected.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{metaregressionCoefficientCorrelationMatrix, }{Include the correlation matrix of the regression coefficients.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{metaregressionCoefficientEstimates, }{Include estimates of the regression coefficients in the meta-regression model.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{metaregressionTermTests, }{Include tests for each term in the meta-regression model. The null hypothesis states that the effect size at all levels of the categorical variable are equal or that there is no linear association between the effect size and the continuous variable).
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{method, }{Method used to estimate heterogeneity in the meta-analysis.}

\item{optimizerConvergenceRelativeTolerance, }{Set the relative convergence tolerance for the optimizer. Available when heterogeneity model terms are included or using specific optimizers in multilevel/multivariate meta-analysis.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{optimizerConvergenceTolerance, }{Set the convergence tolerance for the optimizer. Available when using certain methods without heterogeneity model terms or specific optimizers in multilevel/multivariate meta-analysis.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{optimizerFinalTrustRegionRadius, }{Set the final trust region radius for the optimizer. Available when using specific optimization methods in multilevel/multivariate meta-analysis.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{optimizerInitialTau2, }{Specify the initial value of 𝜏² for the optimization algorithm. Available only for specific optimization methods and unavailable in multilevel/multivariate meta-analysis or when heterogeneity model terms are included.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{optimizerInitialTrustRegionRadius, }{Set the initial trust region radius for the optimizer. Available when using specific optimization methods in multilevel/multivariate meta-analysis.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{optimizerMaximumEvaluations, }{Set the maximum number of function evaluations for the optimizer. Available when using specific optimization methods in multilevel/multivariate meta-analysis.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{optimizerMaximumIterations, }{Set the maximum number of iterations for the optimizer. Available when using certain estimation or optimization methods.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{optimizerMaximumRestarts, }{Set the maximum number of restarts for the optimizer. Available when using the Nelder-Mead method ('nmk') in multilevel/multivariate meta-analysis.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{optimizerMaximumTau2, }{Specify the maximum allowable value of 𝜏² during optimization. Available only for specific optimization methods and unavailable in multilevel/multivariate meta-analysis or when heterogeneity model terms are included.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{optimizerMethod, }{Select the optimization method to use for fitting the model. Available in multilevel/multivariate meta-analysis or when heterogeneity model terms are included.}

\item{optimizerMinimumTau2, }{Specify the minimum allowable value of 𝜏² during optimization. Available only for specific optimization methods and unavailable in multilevel/multivariate meta-analysis or when heterogeneity model terms are included.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{optimizerStepAdjustment, }{Set the step adjustment factor for the optimizer. Available when using certain methods without heterogeneity model terms and unavailable in multilevel/multivariate meta-analysis.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{permutationTest, }{Perform a permutation test for the model coefficients. Available in the meta-analysis analysisType when clustering is not specified. The resulting permuation p-values are displayed in the 'p (permutation)' column. Note that permutation can be computationally intesive.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{permutationTestIteration, }{Specify the number of permutations to use in the approximate permutation test.}

\item{permutationTestType, }{Select the type of permutation test.
\itemize{
  \item \code{"approximate"}
  \item \code{"exact"}
}}

\item{petPeeseCoefficientEstimates, }{Include the estimate of the bias coefficient: the standard error (PET) or the sampling variance (PEESE) coefficient of the reported model.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{petPeeseRegressionPlot, }{Visualize the fitted relationship between the effect sizes and their standard errors. The fitted line (PET) or curve (PEESE) at a standard error of zero corresponds to the adjusted estimate.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{petPeeseThreshold, }{Significance level of the two-sided test of the PET adjusted effect. If the test is significant, PEESE is reported; otherwise PET is reported (Stanley & Doucouliagos, 2014). Available for the conditional PET-PEESE adjustment.}

\item{predictionIntervals, }{Include prediction intervals in the tabular output. Unavailable for unrestricted weighted least squares.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{predictors, }{Variables to include as predictors (moderators) in the meta-regression model.}

\item{randomEffectsConfidenceIntervals, }{Include confidence intervals for the Random Effects / Model Components.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{randomEffectsTestInclusion, }{Test the inclusion of the individual Random Effects / Model Components. The test compares the complete model (i.e., including all components) with a model without one of the specified Random Effects / Model Structure components at a time.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{showMetaforRCode, }{Display the underlying R code used by the metafor package to fit the model.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{standardErrors, }{Include standard errors in the tabular output. Only available when no effect size transformation is applied.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{studyLabels, }{Variable containing labels for the studies. Used for labeling outputs and plots.}

\item{subgroup, }{Variable indicating subgroup stratification. For each subgroup, an independent model is fitted to the corresponding data set subset.}

\item{transformEffectSize, }{Select a transformation to apply to the effect size estimates in the output. This transformation applies to the 'Meta-Analytic Estimates Table', 'Estimated Marginal Means Table', 'Forest Plot', and  the 'Bubble Plot'. The 'Meta-Regression Coeffient Estimates' are not transformed.}

\item{useSparseMatrices, }{Use sparse matrix representations to speed up computations in models with large datasets or complex random effects structures. Available in multilevel/multivariate meta-analysis.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixCheckPositiveDefiniteness, }{Check if the variance-covariance matrix is symmetric.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixCluster, }{Variable specifying clustering of the effect sizes for computing the variance covariance matrix. Effect sizes with different values of the cluster variable are assumed to be independent. Note that this input differs from the 'Clustering' option in the data input which is used to specify cluster-robust standard error. In most cases however, both input should contain the same variable.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixConstruct, }{Variable specifying the construct measured by the effect size. Corresponds to the `obs` option in the metafor's 'vcalc' function.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixConstructType, }{Variable specifying the type of construct measured by the effect size. Construct Type corresponds to a higher level grouping of the Constructs. Corresponds to the `type` option in the metafor's 'vcalc' function.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixCorrelationMatrix, }{Variable specifying the correlation between the individual estimates within cluster. The column order of the variable correspond to the row order of effect size estimates within cluster. Only the lower triangle needs to be specified. Corresponds to the `rvars` option in the metafor's 'vcalc' function.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixFile, }{CSV file containing the precomputed effect size variance-covariance matrix. The matrix needs to match the dimensions of the data set and cannot contain any other variables or names.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixForcePositiveDefiniteness, }{Force the variance-covariance matrix to be positive definite. This option shuld be used with caution as non-positive definite matricies often indicate input misspecification.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixGroup1, }{Variable to specify the group of the first condition when the observed effect sizes or outcomes represent contrasts between two conditions. Corresponds to the `grp1` argument in the metafor's 'vcalc' function.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixGroup2, }{Variable to specify the group of the second condition when the observed effect sizes or outcomes represent contrasts between two conditions. Corresponds to the `grp1` argument in the metafor's 'vcalc' function.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixGroupSize1, }{Variable to specify the size of the group (or more generally, the inverse-sampling variance weight) of the first condition when the observed effect sizes or outcomes represent contrasts between two conditions. Corresponds to the `w1` argument in the metafor's 'vcalc' function.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixGroupSize2, }{Optional numeric vector to specify the size of the group (or more generally, the inverse-sampling variance weight) of the second condition when the observed effect sizes or outcomes represent contrasts between two conditions. Corresponds to the `w2` argument in the metafor's 'vcalc' function.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixSimpleAutoregressiveLag1Correlation, }{Correlation for lag-1 time points in autoregressive structures.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixSimpleAutoregressiveTime, }{Variable specifying the time point of each effect size, required for autoregressive (CAR) structures.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixSimpleCompoundSymmetryConstruct, }{Variable identifying the construct, outcome, or endpoint measured by each effect size. Required for compound symmetry times autoregressive structures.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixSimpleCompoundSymmetryWithinClusterCorrelation, }{Assumed correlation between sampling errors within clusters (compound symmetry).}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixSimpleStructure, }{Parametric correlation structure for sampling errors within clusters. 'Compound symmetry + autoregressive' assumes a constant baseline correlation plus additional correlation that decays over time. 'Compound symmetry × autoregressive' assumes the constant correlation and the time-based decay act jointly, so correlation vanishes at large time lags.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixSubcluster, }{Variable specifying additional structure of the subgroups. Effect sizes within the same cluster with different values of the cluster variable are assumed to be independent. Note that this input corresponds to the 'subgroup' option in the metafor's 'vcalc' function and is renamed for differentiation from subgroup analysis.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixTime1, }{Variable specifying the time point of the effect size measurement. In case multiple time points are specified, the first time corresponds to the first condition.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixTime2, }{Variable specifying the time point of the effect size measurement. In case multiple time points are specified, the second time corresponds to the second condition.}

\item{varianceCovarianceMatrixType, }{Type of variance-covariance matrix input method.
\itemize{
  \item \code{"simple"}: Specify a simple parametric correlation structure for sampling errors within clusters.
  \item \code{"correlationMatrix"}: Use a list of variables to specify the correlation matrix of studies corresponding to the same cluster. Corresponds to the `rvars` option in the metafor's 'vcalc' function.
  \item \code{"constructsGroupsTimes"}: Specify constructs, groups, and times of the measurement to specify the correlation matrix of studies corresponding to the same cluster.
  \item \code{"precomputed"}: Load a csv file containing the precomputed variance-covariance matrix.
}}

\item{weightFunctionCoefficientEstimates, }{Include weight function estimates in the output.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{weightFunctionPValueFrequencyTable, }{Estimate counts within the specified p-value intervals.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{weightFunctionPlot, }{Visualize the relative probability of study being published across the range of p-values.
Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{weightFunctionPlotRescaleXAxis, }{Space p-value thresholds equally along the x-axis.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}

\item{weightedEstimation, }{Perform weighted estimation using inverse-variance weights. Uncheck for unweighted estimation. Unavailable for multilevel/multivariate models, which are always weighted by the inverse of the variance-covariance matrix.
Defaults to \code{TRUE}.}
}
\description{
Classical multilevel/multivariate meta-analysis allows you to conduct a meta-analysis adjusting for the dependency between effect sizes using the classical approach. The effect size dependency can be adjusted for by specifying the 'Effect Size Variance-Covariance Matrix' (interfacing the 'vcalc' function) and specifying the 'Random Effects/Model Components' (interfacing the 'random' argument in 'rma.mv' function). It provides options for fixed and random effects models, as well as meta-regression, and subgroup analysis. Additional options include the ability to specify clustering for robust variance estimation, permutation tests, and generating the metafor package R code. The results include estimates of effect sizes, heterogeneity, moderation, and various plots to visualize the results.
}
\details{
See [this tutorial](https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.09845) for a detailed introduction to the module.
}
